1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Draw a chord across this circle, at random.

2
00:00:03,260 --> 00:00:06,660
What's the chance it's longer than a side of this triangle?

3
00:00:07,260 --> 00:00:08,350
Pick a number.

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:16,810
Bertrand posed this puzzle in eighteen eighty-nine, as a warning about two words: at random.

5
00:00:17,110 --> 00:00:23,000
Leave them vague, and one college's average class is forty, or a hundred forty-seven.

6
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:28,270
Start with the obvious simulation: two random points on the rim, joined.

7
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:32,090
If the chord beats the triangle's side, it turns gold.

8
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:36,823
Ten thousand chords in, the gold share settles near point three three.

9
00:00:37,433 --> 00:00:38,933
Geometry agrees.

10
00:00:39,333 --> 00:00:46,863
With a corner on the first point, the chord is long only when its other end lands on this arc, a third of the rim.

11
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:56,060
Case closed, until you code it a second way: a random radius, a point on it, and the chord through it at right angles.

12
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:58,560
This one settles near point five.

13
00:00:59,166 --> 00:01:03,506
A third program picks any point inside as the chord's midpoint.

14
00:01:03,806 --> 00:01:05,836
That settles near point two five.

15
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:11,093
Bertrand printed all three, writing that none is wrong and none is right.

16
00:01:11,393 --> 00:01:14,203
Each tally is good to about one percentage point.

17
00:01:14,703 --> 00:01:18,273
They disagree by twenty-five, and the code has no bug.

18
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:21,703
One point sets a chord's length: its midpoint.

19
00:01:22,003 --> 00:01:24,303
Slide it outward and the chord shrinks.

20
00:01:24,603 --> 00:01:29,323
It matches the side at half a radius, on the circle inside the triangle.

21
00:01:29,766 --> 00:01:34,626
So a chord is long exactly when its midpoint lands in this gold circle.

22
00:01:34,926 --> 00:01:37,786
Each program is a way of scattering midpoints.

23
00:01:38,233 --> 00:01:40,453
Shrink every chord to its midpoint.

24
00:01:40,753 --> 00:01:47,743
The midpoint program fills the disk evenly, so this circle, a quarter of the area, catches a quarter.

25
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:52,093
The radius program spreads them evenly along the radius.

26
00:01:52,393 --> 00:01:53,703
Where will they crowd?

27
00:01:55,203 --> 00:02:00,223
Toward the center: every ring gets an equal share, and inner rings are small.

28
00:02:00,523 --> 00:02:02,473
Half the radius: one half.

29
00:02:03,066 --> 00:02:09,756
The endpoints program piles them against the rim: only a third land inside, the third we found.

30
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:15,180
Three programs, three things spread evenly: rim, radius, area.

31
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:17,490
At random never said which.

32
00:02:18,833 --> 00:02:22,193
Edwin Jaynes argued a physical process picks one.

33
00:02:22,693 --> 00:02:30,273
Straws tossed from a standing height can't be aimed, so the answer can't depend on where the circle sits, or its size.

34
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:32,843
One rain lands on both circles.

35
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:38,453
Each judges with its own gold circle, so a fair program scores the same on both.

36
00:02:38,753 --> 00:02:41,623
Midpoint: a quarter, then point four one.

37
00:02:42,623 --> 00:02:45,603
Endpoints: a third, then point four seven.

38
00:02:46,603 --> 00:02:49,433
Only the radius program gives a half on both.

39
00:02:50,766 --> 00:02:55,126
Jaynes and Charles Tyler tossed real straws at a five-inch circle.

40
00:02:55,426 --> 00:02:59,296
The hundred twenty-eight that crossed it matched the radius program.

41
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:02,753
Surveys hide the same two words.

42
00:03:03,053 --> 00:03:05,873
Take classes of ten, ten and a hundred.

43
00:03:06,373 --> 00:03:09,403
Pick a class at random: the average is forty.

44
00:03:09,903 --> 00:03:12,603
Pick a student instead, and it's eighty-five.

45
00:03:13,103 --> 00:03:17,823
At Stony Brook, the same two picks gave forty and a hundred forty-seven.

46
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:22,706
Whatever number you picked, some way of drawing chords makes it right.

47
00:03:23,006 --> 00:03:30,286
Name the way, and the answer is fixed: a half for tossed straws, a hundred forty-seven for a random student.

